fact WorkItem(id: "WI-746", description: some(value: "DESIGN: make anthill data files OPT-IN instead of discovered by directory slurp — the residual WI-744 could not close. TODAY collect_data_files (anthill-cli/src/main.rs) recursively claims EVERY .toml/.json under any path handed to load/check/query/prove/codegen, descending into target/, node_modules/, .git/ and hidden dirs, and treats each as anthill data. That conflates 'in the directory' with 'addressed to us'. There is also NO way to opt out or in: collect_anthill_files REJECTS an explicitly-named .toml ('not an .anthill file'), so directory recursion is the ONLY channel by which a data file can be supplied.\n\nWI-744 made the load arm loud and that immediately exposed the conflation: an ordinary Cargo.toml gave 'error: missing meta: section has no meta key' and killed the load (VERIFIED live). The stopgap shipped in WI-744 is a SNIFF — term_ser::is_anthill_data / load_data_file — testing for the meta+data envelope: no envelope means not ours, so it is neither loaded nor complained about. That fixes the reported regression and is right as far as it goes.\n\nTHE RESIDUAL IT CANNOT CLOSE: intent is unobservable when data is DISCOVERED rather than declared. Two consequences, both accepted deliberately in WI-744 and both recorded at term_ser::load_data_file:\n  (1) A file that will not PARSE yields no envelope, hence no evidence it was ever ours, so it must be skipped silently. We tried the alternative (parse failure = Err) and it is untenable: a .vscode/settings.json or tsconfig.json with JSONC comments is not valid strict JSON, so it killed 'anthill load' for every VS Code user over a file the tool merely FOUND (VERIFIED: 'error: .../settings.json: format error: key must be a string at line 2 column 3', exit 1). So a file genuinely INTENDED as data, with a syntax error bad enough to stop parsing, is now dropped without a word — a silent skip, in the ticket whose whole point was to kill silent skips.\n  (2) A data file whose author forgot the meta envelope entirely reads as foreign and is likewise ignored.\nBoth dissolve the moment data is OPT-IN: 'the user said this is data' is the evidence a parse failure can be reported against. Sniffing is a proxy for intent; a declaration IS intent.\n\nLIKELY SHAPE (decide): (a) name data files on the command line — needs collect_anthill_files to stop rejecting non-.anthill paths and to route them to the data channel instead, which is a small change and gives an exact answer; (b) a conventional location (<project>/data/*.toml) auto-loaded, nothing else scanned; (c) a manifest/import declaring the project's data files, which is the most anthill-ish (the program says what it depends on) and composes with 007-persistence-layer. (a) and (b) are compatible. Whatever lands, the recursive unfiltered slurp goes away, and with it the descent into target/ (1585 .json files in this repo's own target/ today).\n\nKEEP the envelope sniff regardless — it is a cheap correctness guard for the conventional-location case, where a foreign file can still turn up. But once intent is declared, a NAMED data file that fails to parse must be a LOUD error again.\n\nACCEPTANCE: a .toml/.json is loaded as anthill data only when the user has said it is (named, conventional location, or declared); a NAMED data file that fails to parse or lacks the envelope is a loud error (intent is known, so silence is wrong); an unrelated Cargo.toml / package.json / .vscode/settings.json / target/**.json is never read, regardless of where it sits; anthill-cli/tests/load_cmd_test.rs's foreign_data_files_are_not_our_business still passes, and broken_data_file_blocks_the_load is extended to cover an unparseable NAMED data file; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-18T07:02:39Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-746", author: "user", content: "SNIFF REFINED during WI-744 verify (2026-07-17): is_anthill_data now keys on meta.entity being a STRING, not on a bare 'meta' key. Keying on the bare key still over-claimed: a foreign TOML with [meta]/[data] sections, and a JSON with a nested 'meta' object, have no entity string yet were claimed and errored — the SAME spurious-block class as Cargo.toml, in a rarer shape (VERIFIED live: both produced 'error: ...: missing meta: meta.entity must be a string', exit 1; now both load clean). meta.entity is what load_section actually requires and what every real data file carries, so tightening rejects no loadable file. Pinned by anthill-cli/tests/fixtures/load/foreign-files/{config.toml,manifest.json} + foreign_data_files_are_not_our_business.\n\nTHE RESIDUAL THIS TICKET STILL OWNS (now a false-NEGATIVE, not a false-positive): a file GENUINELY meant as data but missing a string meta.entity reads as not-ours and is skipped SILENTLY. Acceptable only because such a file would not load anyway (load_section errors on the identical condition), so nothing loadable is dropped — but it is still a silent skip, and it is the same shape as the parse-failure residual already recorded here. Both dissolve under opt-in: once data is DECLARED, 'the user said this is data' is the evidence that turns the skip back into a loud error. So this ticket's acceptance already covers it — no new work, just a second concrete instance of why sniff-by-shape cannot be the final answer.", at: "2026-07-17T15:02:32Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-746", author: "user", content: "REVIEW PASS (xhigh, 8 findings) — one REAL BUG found in my own first cut, now fixed.\n\nTHE BUG (self-inflicted, and the ticket's own thesis violated): the collector probed candidates with `candidate.is_file()`. That answers false not only for 'absent' but for a DANGLING SYMLINK, a directory wearing the name, and any stat failure — so a DECLARED data file that could not be read vanished with no diagnostic, exit 0, facts missing. VERIFIED LIVE both ways: dangling symlink at anthill.toml gave 'loaded: 2246 facts', exit 0. It was also a REGRESSION — the extension-walk it replaced collected such a path and let read_to_string fail loudly. Note fs_util.rs:58 tolerates the identical is_dir() gap, but its stated reason ('a dangling symlink would become a hard error — over-claiming files that were never ours') INVERTS at a conventional name, where the file is ours by definition. FIX: only io::ErrorKind::NotFound counts as absent; anything else is collected and the load site's read_to_string reports what is actually wrong. symlink_metadata not metadata, since following the link turns a dangling symlink back into NotFound and reopens the hole. Now: 'error: .../anthill.toml: read error: No such file or directory', exit 1; genuinely-absent still silent.\n\nTEST GAP (also fixed): all 6 original tests pinned a REFUSAL (blocked, or not-read) — NONE asserted that a valid anthill.toml's facts actually reach the KB. A regression that found the file and dropped its facts would have passed the whole suite. Added declared_data_reaches_the_kb (queries them back, not just a count — a count also rises if facts land malformed), unreadable_data_file_blocks_the_load (directory-named fixture; git cannot portably store a dangling symlink, both ride the same two lines), both_conventional_formats_load.\n\nOPEN, DELIBERATELY NOT DONE (judgement calls, not oversights):\n 1. ORDERING DEPENDENCY: conventional_data_files is silent on a missing/unstattable path and is safe only because collect_anthill_files runs first and rejects those. Documented, not enforced by the signature. A reordering or a second caller reinstates the bug class.\n 2. REUSE: anthill-todo/src/main.rs:137 already does PROJECT_MARKERS.iter().any(|m| dir.join(m).is_file()) — structurally the same 'known names in a directory' probe, INCLUDING the same is_file() gap now fixed here. Second copy of the pattern WI-747 spent a ticket collapsing; the is_file() fix arguably belongs there too.\n 3. ALTITUDE: 'which files constitute a project' landed in anthill-cli, not the fs_util home WI-747 established for exactly that. Counter-precedent is real (WI-747 kept POLICY wrappers per-binary, and PROJECT_MARKERS is per-binary), so this is defensible — flagged so it is a choice, not a default.\n 4. BUNDLE ASYMMETRY (pre-existing, now sharper): run_codegen_bundle collects only .anthill and never reaches conventional_data_files, so a project's anthill.toml facts are present under load/check/query and ABSENT from the generated crate. Predates this change; invisible today because no project uses data files. Needs a decision, possibly its own ticket.\n\nFULL WORKSPACE: 83 suites, 2973 passed, 0 failed via scripts/test.sh. NOT committed.", at: "2026-07-18T06:58:21Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-746", author: "user", content: "FOLLOW-UP DELIVERED (same ticket, not spun off): `anthill run` now reads the project's conventional data files.\n\nThe review's finding #4 was THREE claims and only ONE held. Corrected by reading the code rather than assuming:\n - `codegen rust` — NOT AFFECTED. generate_rust(&ParsedFile) per file, never builds a KB; it emits structs/enums from sort/entity DECLARATIONS. Data files carry FACTS, which play no part. I was wrong to list it.\n - `codegen bundle` — SHOULD NOT read, and the original claim was wrong on the merits. It vendors the program's .anthill SOURCE TEXT into a standalone crate. Data is the program's INPUT, not its code; baking anthill.toml in at bundle time FREEZES it, inverting the point of the .toml/.json channel (021: 'written by Tools' — data that changes independently of the logic). A bundled binary needing data should read it at ITS runtime — a separate capability, not a bundling gap.\n - `anthill run` — THE REAL GAP, and I had told the user it was fine. run::build_kb assembles its own KB (parse_user_files -> load_all) instead of going through load_kb_with_stdlib, so it never reached the data path. Same directory answered differently under `anthill query` (facts present) and `anthill run` (absent) — and unlike the bundle, run re-reads every invocation so nothing freezes.\n\nFIX: extracted the data-load block out of load_kb_with_stdlib into `load_conventional_data(kb, paths, verbose) -> Result<(), Vec<String>>`; both it and run::build_kb call it. Returns faults rather than printing+exiting because the callers use different exit codes (1 vs runner::EXIT_COMPILE). Extracted rather than copied — a second copy is exactly the WI-747 divergence, and the ORIGIN of this bug is that the KB was assembled in two places.\n\nTESTS: run_cmd_test +2 (11 -> 13). broken_data_blocks_the_run probes the WIRING (if run stops reading data the fixture is ignored and the program exits 0); valid_data_does_not_disturb_the_run is the control. What the facts DO once loaded stays pinned once, against the shared helper, by load_cmd_test::declared_data_reaches_the_kb — no reason to rebuild pattern_query machinery to re-test the same function. VERIFIED LIVE both ways before/after.\n\n021 updated: command list corrected to load/check/query/run/codegen-cpp, plus an explicit note on why codegen rust and codegen bundle abstain.\n\nFull workspace: 83 suites, 2975 passed, 0 failed. Remaining open findings from the review pass are unchanged (ordering dependency; anthill-todo:137 is_file gap; fs_util placement).", at: "2026-07-18T07:16:05Z")
